text:
Oh! the old, old clock, of the household stock;
Was the brightest thing and neatest;
Its hands though old, had touch of gold,
And its chime still rang the sweetest;
Twas monitor too, though its words were few,
Yet they lived though nations altered;
And its voice still strong, warned old and young,
When the voice of friendship faltered;
Tick-tick it said; quick, quick 70 bad:
For ten I've given warning;
Up. up-and go-or else, you know,


You'll never rise soon in the morning!
A friendly voice was that old, old clock,


AS it stood in the corner smiling,
And it blessed the ii me- with a merry chime,
The wintry hours beguiling;
But cross old voice was that tircsome clock,
AS it called at aybreak boldly,
When the dawn looked gray o'er the misty way,
And the early air blew coIdly;
Tick-tick it said; quick out of bed,


For five 1've given warning
You'll never have health, you'll never have


wealth,


Unless you're up soon in the morning!
Still hourly the sound goes round and round,
With tone that ceases never;
While tears are shed for the bright days fed,
And the old friends lost forever.
Its heart beats on though hearts are gone,
That warmer beat and stronger;
Its hands still move-though hands we love,
Are clasped on earth no longer!
Tick-tick it said; to the church Jard bed;
The grave hath given warning;
Then up and rise, and look to the skies,
And prepare for heavenly morning.
